---

<CaseStudy
  title="Hunziker Areal"
  subtitle="The co-op courtyard logic, scaled to a neighborhood."
  heroSrc="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/HAL%20014243.jpg?width=1400"
  heroPlaceholder="Hunziker Areal hero — square between the houses (photo needed)"
  heroCredit="Haller Juliet · CC BY-SA 4.0"
  heroCreditHref="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HAL_014243.jpg"
  facts={facts}
  gallery={gallery}
  prev={{ href: '/vauban/', label: 'Vauban' }}
  next={{ href: '/gleis-21/', label: 'Gleis 21' }}
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] px-6 pt-16 sm:px-12">
    <p class="text-[21px] leading-[1.62]">
      Zurich's cooperatives pooled their experience into one flagship: thirteen buildings on a
      former concrete plant, roughly 370 homes for some 1,200 people, with squares and lanes between
      the houses instead of one enclosed court. Ground floors carry workshops, a guest house,
      restaurants, and studios; upstairs, everything from studios to large cluster apartments.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-6 text-[21px] leading-[1.62]">
      What to steal: the coalition as client. "Mehr als wohnen" — more than housing — was founded by
      dozens of co-ops precisely to test ideas none could risk alone. It is the institutional form a
      second- and third-building Berkeley would need.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="lessons">
    <li>
      A coalition of co-ops can hold land and risk that no single group can — build the umbrella
      early.
    </li>
    <li>Thirteen buildings by different architects, one brief: coherence without monoculture.</li>
    <li>Cluster apartments serve households the unit-mix spreadsheet forgets.</li>
    <li>The pilot-project mindset — test, publish, repeat — is itself the export.</li>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="sources">
    Sources:
    <a href="https://www.mehralswohnen.ch" target="_blank" rel="noopener">mehralswohnen.ch</a>.
    Figures approximate.
  </Fragment>
</CaseStudy>
